WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.125
Meet Q B C Grid, a focused Angular library for desktop dashboards.

00:00:04.125 --> 00:00:06.531
The grid owns tile placement and interaction.

00:00:06.531 --> 00:00:10.313
Your application supplies the widgets and decides where layouts are stored.

00:00:10.313 --> 00:00:16.501
This walkthrough uses the real Angular application, with a presentation frame and live measurements added for the recording.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.183
Enter edit mode and drag a tile into free space.

00:00:23.183 --> 00:00:28.276
The tile lifts under the pointer, while a separate shadow previews its destination in whole cells.

00:00:28.276 --> 00:00:31.460
Grid lines appear during the gesture and disappear on release.

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:34.007
One committed layout change reaches the host application.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.667
An occupied destination shows an invalid, dashed preview.

00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:47.002
Releasing the pointer restores the original placement without pushing or swapping neighboring tiles.

00:00:47.002 --> 00:00:50.670
You can also press Escape during a drag to cancel it.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:54.671
Neither a refused move nor a cancelled gesture publishes a changed layout.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.511
Drag the corner handle to resize a tile.

00:01:02.511 --> 00:01:07.533
Its top left corner stays fixed while the destination snaps to whole column and row spans.

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:10.044
Each tile can declare minimum and maximum sizes.

00:01:10.044 --> 00:01:15.066
Here, growth stops at five columns and four rows, then shrinking stops at two by two.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.922
Live mode removes layout editing affordances while widget controls remain usable.

00:01:23.922 --> 00:01:27.132
Edit mode enables movement and resizing for unlocked tiles.

00:01:27.132 --> 00:01:30.698
Locking a tile removes its resize handle and blocks movement.

00:01:30.698 --> 00:01:32.482
Unlock it to restore editing.

00:01:32.482 --> 00:01:35.691
The host application owns these mode and lock controls.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:44.195
Focus an unlocked tile and use the arrow keys to move one cell at a time.

00:01:44.195 --> 00:01:46.030
Hold Shift with an arrow to resize.

00:01:46.030 --> 00:01:49.176
The grid announces the resulting position or refusal through a live region.

00:01:49.176 --> 00:01:52.847
The panel on the right mirrors that actual announcement so you can see it.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.040
Sometimes a single cell step cannot cross an obstacle.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:06.756
This locked tile spans every column, so Arrow Down is refused.

00:02:06.756 --> 00:02:10.810
Control plus Arrow Down finds the nearest fitting position beyond the barrier.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:13.175
Control plus Arrow Up returns the tile.

00:02:13.175 --> 00:02:16.891
Keyboard users can reach the same free regions as pointer users.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:24.985
The Add tile control asks the grid to choose the first available position in row order.

00:02:24.985 --> 00:02:28.723
Applications can also call add Tile with explicit coordinates, as shown here.

00:02:28.723 --> 00:02:31.216
Removing a tile leaves its neighbors in place.

00:02:31.216 --> 00:02:35.266
When a focused tile is removed, focus moves to a nearby editable tile.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:42.023
Tiles contain real projected Angular components.

00:02:42.023 --> 00:02:45.734
Type a note and scroll the readings, then move the tile.

00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:48.770
The note, scroll position, and component instance remain intact.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:55.516
Input fields own their keyboard and pointer events, so interacting with a widget does not accidentally start a grid gesture.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.081
Layouts are plain records with tile identifiers, cell coordinates, spans, and optional metadata.

00:03:05.081 --> 00:03:10.944
After a move, this host stores the emitted layout as JSON in browser local storage.

00:03:10.944 --> 00:03:12.899
Reloading restores the same placement.

00:03:12.899 --> 00:03:18.371
Storage belongs to the application; the published grid has no persistence or backend dependency.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:25.003
Stored layouts can contain invalid records, duplicate identifiers, impossible dimensions, or overlapping tiles.

00:03:25.003 --> 00:03:28.852
The grid normalizes the supplied data in a deterministic order.

00:03:28.852 --> 00:03:34.241
Here, unusable records disappear, dimensions are clamped, and the later overlapping tile moves down.

00:03:34.241 --> 00:03:37.705
The repaired layout is then emitted to the host.

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:43.820
The grid responds to its container width without changing its column count.

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:47.640
Watch the measured width change while the layout remains twelve columns wide.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.868
Tiles still use the same cell coordinates.

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:54.961
Moving a tile into lower rows extends the dashboard vertically; the host provides the scrolling region.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.664
The separate Q B C design system owns the grid's CSS custom properties.

00:04:04.664 --> 00:04:08.969
Override those tokens to change surfaces, borders, focus color, and interaction previews.

00:04:08.969 --> 00:04:11.840
The same behavior works with the new theme.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:17.580
A reduced motion preference sets the overlay and settling durations to zero without changing interaction rules.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.930
This dashboard contains sixty tiles.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:27.723
Dragging still previews a single destination, while the committed change counter stays steady until release.

00:04:27.723 --> 00:04:32.744
The implementation schedules visual updates by animation frame and avoids moving neighboring tiles.

00:04:32.744 --> 00:04:38.151
This is a functional demonstration; the repository's performance tests provide the separate measurement checks.

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.209
The package exposes a standalone grid component, a tile template directive, and plain layout types.

00:04:45.209 --> 00:04:49.029
Angular is a peer dependency, and the design tokens ship separately.

00:04:49.029 --> 00:04:55.280
Start with the README for setup, the API guide for integration, and the contribution guide to get involved.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.711
